 Task frame stage 4. The Cobblestone button is the first child of the band, so
 it moves to whichever edge the band is docked to, and the menu must open at
 the button. Before the fix `desk-menu-tree` took no `ds` and could not know
 the edge: a flex-1 spacer above the body anchored it to the BOTTOM and one
 beside the column anchored it to the LEFT, at every edge.

 THE CLAIM IS THE GAP TO THE BAND, and it took two wrong assertions to get
 here. Both are recorded because both are the same mistake and it is an easy
 one to make again.

 The first asked whether the menu's left edge EQUALS the button's. It reads NO
 even at the bottom edge, where the placement is correct, because the theme
 pads the band's button by 14 and the menu column by 16. An assertion whose
 honest answer is a near miss is not an assertion.

 The second asked whether the menu's CENTRE is on the same half of the glass
 as the button's. That survived until the list learned to run across, and then
 read NO at both vertical edges for a menu placed correctly: a 544 wide menu
 beside a left-docked band has its centre past the midline while its near edge
 sits against the band. A verdict about a midpoint is not a verdict about a
 placement.

 What "the menu opens at the button" actually means is that the menu sits
 against the band, on the inside, at whichever edge the band is on. That is
 one signed number per edge, the gap along the band's own axis, and it is
 printed rather than only judged. It fails loudly for the defect this file was
 written for: a menu at the opposite corner is not a near miss, it is hundreds
 of pixels.

 THE BUTTON'S HEIGHT IS ASKED SEPARATELY AND IS NOT A CONSEQUENCE OF THE
 ANCHOR. A vertical band lays the same button through the same code path, and
 a height of zero would mean a control that can be neither seen nor hit. It
 gets its own column here rather than a verdict folded into the anchor rows,
 because a fix for one has no reason to move the other.

 THE ORIENTATION IS ASKED AS AN AGREEMENT rather than as a shape, because the
 claim is that the list follows the BAND: across when the band runs down, down
 when it runs across. Asking only "is it wide" would pass at two of the four
 edges for a list that never changed, and the printed w and h carry the actual
 numbers for a reader who wants them.

 SHUTDOWN IS ASKED THE SAME WAY AND FOR THE SAME REASON. A panel stretches its
 children on the cross axis, so the button is the menu's full width down the
 page, which is right and matches the rows, and was the full span of five
 group columns across, which is not. The verdict is the agreement rather than
 a width, because a fixed number here would be a second copy of the theme's
 text metrics and would move every time the face did.

 IT IS ALSO ASKED WHETHER THE BUTTON EXISTS, and that column is not
 defensive: the first attempt at the fix laid it out at width 0, because its
 minimum width was zero and a spacer beside it took every remaining pixel.
 "Narrower than the menu" was TRUE of that button, so the agreement column
 alone scored an invisible, unclickable control as a pass. A shrink test needs
 a floor or it is a deletion test.

 THE MEASURE IS THE SLACK AND NOT "NARROWER THAN THE MENU", which was the
 third near-miss assertion this file has had to unlearn. A STRETCHED button is
 already a little narrower than its parent, because the theme pads it: 144
 inside 156 down the page. Asking "is it narrower" therefore answers yes in
 both orientations and distinguishes nothing. The slack does: 12 when the
 button fills its parent, 440 when it keeps its own text width beside a row of
 five group columns. The threshold is padding-sized rather than a fraction, so
 it says what it means.

Section: Body

  dma-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dma-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

  dma-box : WidgetNode, Text -> LayoutRect
  dma-box (laid) (id) =
    when widget-find laid id
      is Just (n) -> n.wn-bounds
      is None -> layout-rect 0 0 0 0

  dma-laid : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dma-laid (ds) (w) (h) (s) =
    widget-layout (desk-chrome-with (desk-menu-tree (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) gfont-none s ds) w ds) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))

  dma-rect : Text, LayoutRect -> Text
  dma-rect (nm) (r) =
    " " & nm & " x " & integer-to-text (r.lr-x) & " y " & integer-to-text (r.lr-y) & " w " & integer-to-text (r.lr-w) & " h " & integer-to-text (r.lr-h)

  dma-mid-y : LayoutRect -> Integer
  dma-mid-y (r) = r.lr-y + r.lr-h / 2

  dma-mid-x : LayoutRect -> Integer
  dma-mid-x (r) = r.lr-x + r.lr-w / 2

 Positive is a space between the two, negative is an overlap, and both are
 wrong past a threshold. The menu is allowed to sit a little off the band
 because the theme pads it; it is not allowed to sit under it or across the
 glass from it.

  dma-gap : LayoutRect, LayoutRect, Integer -> Integer
  dma-gap (men) (band) (e) =
    if e == dk-edge-bottom then band.lr-y - (men.lr-y + men.lr-h)
    else if e == dk-edge-top then men.lr-y - (band.lr-y + band.lr-h)
    else if e == dk-edge-left then men.lr-x - (band.lr-x + band.lr-w)
    else band.lr-x - (men.lr-x + men.lr-w)

  dma-row : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dma-row (w) (h) (e) =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed dk-ds-bytes 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pp = poke-32 ds desk-prog-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell e
    in let tki = dk-task-init ds gfont-none w
    in let sti = dk-strip-init ds gfont-none w
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = dma-laid ds w h s
    in let btn = dma-box laid "menu"
    in let men = dma-box laid "sysmenu-body"
    in let gap = dma-gap men (dma-box laid "taskbar") e
    in "edge " & dk-edge-name e
       & dma-rect "button" btn
       & dma-rect "menu" men
       & "  gap to the band " & integer-to-text gap
       & "  against it " & dma-yn (gap >= 0 & gap <= 32)
       & "  button has height " & dma-yn (btn.lr-h > 0)
       & "  list runs across " & dma-yn ((men.lr-w > men.lr-h) == dk-task-vertical ds)
       & "  shutdown w " & integer-to-text (dma-box laid "shutdown").lr-w
       & "  is real " & dma-yn ((dma-box laid "shutdown").lr-w > 0)
       & "  slack " & integer-to-text (men.lr-w - (dma-box laid "shutdown").lr-w)
       & "  sized to itself " & dma-yn ((men.lr-w - (dma-box laid "shutdown").lr-w > 32) == dk-task-vertical ds)

 THE ROWS ABOVE RUN FACELESS AND CANNOT SEE THE ENTRY'S SIZE. `dk-task-btn-h`
 asks the face for a line's height and answers the bitmap cell when there is
 none, so the two orientations agreed at 16 with no face and still disagreed on
 the glass, which is where Damian saw it. The entry also takes its own natural
 height as a floor now, so the number this row reports is 24 rather than 16.
 This row supplies a face the way `desk-taskbar-hit` does, a plain record with
 `gf-ok` true and an 18 pixel cell, and prints the entry's height at all four
 edges on one line so the comparison is a comparison rather than four numbers
 to hold in the head.

  dma-face : GopFont
  dma-face = GopFont { gf-ok = True, gf-base = 0, gf-gw = 0, gf-gh = 18, gf-asc = 0, gf-cap = 0 }

  dma-faced-h : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dma-faced-h (w) (h) (e) =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed dk-ds-bytes 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pp = poke-32 ds desk-prog-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let pei = dk-pedge-init ds
    in let sp = poke-32 ds dk-task-edge-cell e
    in let tki = dk-task-init ds dma-face w
    in let sti = dk-strip-init ds dma-face w
    in let s = ui-wscale w
    in let laid = widget-layout (desk-chrome-face (desk-menu-tree (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) dma-face s ds) w ds dma-face) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in (dma-box laid "menu").lr-h

  dma-faced : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dma-faced (w) (h) =
    let b = dma-faced-h w h dk-edge-bottom
    in let t = dma-faced-h w h dk-edge-top
    in let l = dma-faced-h w h dk-edge-left
    in let r = dma-faced-h w h dk-edge-right
    in "faced entry height: bottom " & integer-to-text b & "  top " & integer-to-text t
       & "  left " & integer-to-text l & "  right " & integer-to-text r
       & "  the sides match the ends " & dma-yn (l == b & r == b)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni (dma-row 1600 900 dk-edge-bottom)
      print-line-uni (dma-row 1600 900 dk-edge-top)
      print-line-uni (dma-row 1600 900 dk-edge-left)
      print-line-uni (dma-row 1600 900 dk-edge-right)
      print-line-uni (dma-faced 1600 900)
    end

